  <h1>Does machine learning make portfolios better?</h1>

  <p class="standfirst">We tested three techniques on a portfolio of ten large US stocks.
  One of them helped. One did nothing at all. And simply splitting the money evenly across
  all ten beat every clever version on returns.</p>

  <p class="byline">Jackline Jebet, with Edgar Jandi and Tomiwo Ebenezer Akinbowale
  &middot; MSc Financial Engineering, WorldQuant University</p>

  <div class="answer">
    <h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p>Sometimes, and less often than you would think.</p>
    <p>Machine learning helped us take smaller losses. It did not help us make more money.
    And in one case it changed nothing whatsoever, for a reason that had nothing to do with
    the technique.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What we were trying to do</h2>

  <p>Say you have money to invest across ten companies. How much should go into each one?</p>

  <p>That is the whole question. Put too much in one place and a single bad year hurts you
  badly. Spread it too thin and you own a little of everything with no real conviction. The
  field of portfolio management exists to answer this, and it has produced some genuinely
  clever mathematics over the last seventy years.</p>

  <p>Recently, machine learning techniques have been added to the toolkit. The promise is
  that they handle messy real-world data better than the older methods. So we tested three
  of them properly and compared the results against the simplest possible approach: put ten
  percent in each stock and leave it alone.</p>

  <h2>What we found</h2>

  <p>We ran every version across five separate time periods, rather than one. This matters
  more than it sounds, and I will come back to it.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Approach</th><th>Average growth</th><th>Worst fall</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr class="hl"><td>Split evenly across all ten</td><td>39.0%</td><td>&minus;9.7%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Grouping similar stocks together</td><td>34.2%</td><td>&minus;7.5%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Grouping, plus cleaning the data first</td><td>33.2%</td><td>&minus;7.7%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Standard optimisation</td><td>33.3%</td><td>&minus;10.6%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Standard optimisation, plus cleaning</td><td>32.2%</td><td>&minus;10.7%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="tnote">Average annual growth rate and the worst peak-to-trough fall, averaged
  across five separate test periods.</p>

  <p>The even split won on growth. No mathematics, no estimation, no code beyond dividing
  by ten.</p>

  <p>However, look at the second column. The grouping method took noticeably smaller falls,
  around seven and a half percent against nearly eleven percent for the standard approach.
  So it did do something real. It just did it on the losing side rather than the winning
  side.</p>

  <p class="pull">Machine learning did not make us more money. It made the bad periods less
  painful, which is a smaller claim and a more honest one.</p>

  <h2>The technique that changed nothing</h2>

  <p>One of the three did absolutely nothing, and working out why took a while.</p>

  <p>The technique cleans up statistical noise in the data before the calculation runs. It
  worked exactly as described. We measured the improvement and the data really was cleaner
  afterwards. Then we compared the portfolio it produced against the one we already had,
  and they were identical. Not similar. Identical.</p>

  <p>The reason turned out to be a rule we had set at the very beginning. No single holding
  could be more than twenty percent of the portfolio. That sounds like a boring
  administrative detail, and it is the sort of rule a real investment mandate carries all
  the time.</p>

  <p>But with a twenty percent ceiling, you need at least five holdings to add up to one
  hundred percent. Our calculation picked exactly five, and put every one of them at the
  ceiling. There was nowhere else for it to go. So improving the data underneath could not
  change the answer, because the answer had already been decided by the rule.</p>

  <p>Which means we had spent our effort improving something that was never the constraint.</p>

  <h2>Why testing once is not enough</h2>

  <p>Here is the part I would most want a non-technical reader to take away.</p>

  <p>If we had tested on a single three-month period, as a lot of published work does, we
  would have reported that the best machine learning version won on nearly every measure.
  It did. That period looked completely convincing.</p>

  <p>Across all five periods, the picture fell apart. The results varied so much from one
  period to the next that the differences between the methods stopped meaning anything. In
  one of the five periods, four of our six portfolios actually lost money on a risk-adjusted
  basis.</p>

  <p>So a single test tells you about that period. It does not tell you about the method.
  Anyone showing you one impressive backtest is showing you one roll of the dice.</p>

  <hr class="rule">

  <h2>What we would actually recommend</h2>

  <p>If you care mainly about growth, the evidence here says the even split is hard to beat,
  and it costs nothing to implement. This is not a new finding. Researchers published the
  same result in 2009 and it has held up repeatedly since.</p>

  <p>If you care about limiting losses, the grouping method earned its place. It consistently
  took smaller falls, it is about forty lines of code, and it needs no forecast of which
  stocks will do well.</p>

  <p>And before reaching for any of it, check what is actually driving your outcome. In our
  case it was a rule written before any of the analysis started. No amount of sophistication
  could move a result that something else had already fixed.</p>

  <p class="pull">Diagnose first. Most of the value was in working out which thing was
  binding, not in applying the cleverest tool.</p>

  <p>Machine learning in portfolio management is not a scam and it is not a revolution. It
  is a set of tools that work under particular conditions. Knowing which conditions you are
  in is worth more than knowing the tools.</p>

